    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Latitudes are parallel circles running east-west, measuring angular distance north or south from the Equator (0°).",
        "Longitudes are vertical meridians extending from pole to pole, measuring angular distance east or west from the Prime Meridian (0°).",
        "Earth rotates 360° in 24 hours, corresponding to 15° per hour or 1° every 4 minutes.",
        "Indian Standard Time (IST) is based on 82.5° E longitude, which is 5 hours and 30 minutes ahead of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT).",
        "The International Date Line roughly follows the 180° meridian, deviating to avoid crossing landmasses and island groups."
      ],
